        <h2 id="section-20">
          When Should You Seek Emergency Medical Attention?
        </h2>
        <p>
          Some withdrawal symptoms and substance-related emergencies require
          immediate medical care.
        </p>
        <p>
          Call 911 or go to the nearest emergency department if someone
          experiences:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Seizures.</li>
          <li>Hallucinations or severe confusion.</li>
          <li>Difficulty breathing.</li>
          <li>Loss of consciousness.</li>
          <li>Severe chest pain.</li>
          <li>Uncontrollable vomiting with signs of dehydration.</li>
          <li>Extreme agitation or dangerous behavior.</li>
          <li>Suspected drug overdose.</li>
          <li>
            If an opioid overdose is suspected, administer naloxone if
            available, call 911, and follow emergency instructions.
          </li>
          <li>
            Do not wait for a routine outpatient appointment when someone has
            potentially life-threatening symptoms.
          </li>
        </ul>
